\paragraph{Pathway compensation under selective intervention.}
Obs-decoder-only SGS reduces CIVO incidence from 6/6 (untreated, two environments) to 1/4 on Walker Walk, compared with 0/3 elimination for full-pathway SGS---confirming the observation decoder as the primary but not sole driver. The single failure (seed~4, $\nve = \texttt{TBD}\times$ at 65K) exhibits a delayed-onset trajectory absent under full gradient flow, consistent with compensatory amplification: with observation decoder gradients blocked, the reward head (38--49\% of gradient magnitude) becomes the dominant adaptation signal to $e_{\dt}$ and may drive co-adaptation through a channel that was subordinate under full gradient flow. This interpretation finds symmetric support in reward-only SGS (exp\_016e), where blocking the reward gradient produced catastrophic CIVO ($\nve = 10.42\times$), consistent with the observation decoder channel amplifying when reward feedback is removed. The pattern---blocking either pathway individually shifts co-adaptation to the remaining one---indicates that CIVO operates through redundant gradient channels, and that targeted intervention is preferable to indiscriminate blocking. Full-pathway SGS eliminates all non-KL gradient pathways yet achieves 0/3 CIVO elimination, establishing forward-pass co-adaptation as the irreducible vulnerability floor that no gradient-level intervention can address.

